1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\text{ }\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}\\,\\,\\,\\,[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 15\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -2 & -1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{60}[\\begin{aligned}15 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy 