ng coefficient matrix are linearly independent.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-7 \\\\ -{x}_{1}-2{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 3{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=12\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}}=\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}]}}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -2 \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -3 & -2 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-7 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 12\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}14 & -16 \\\\ -16 & 29\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{150}[\\begin{aligned}29 & 16 \\\\ 16 & 14\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}26 \\\\ -19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-41780/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "74efec6b-6e5f-5761-a116-5c94e3fb4326"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-41780"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-85280/selection/6cf1ba8fcecf7ff74fcb48e7f39a2cabed6551908e76b1f229ada74cff9b7d1c" :answer/id #uuid "384c4911-96bd-5d4f-915b-7e88f6c052b3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{7}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85280/selection/4a34f9e3c0a8895beb5f43363c365da07fa4da2c2c1f10b145640ba26f13b959" :answer/id #uuid "22428b20-a26b-5085-bb46-c01057a2fa7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{71}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85280/selection/d21cf04195b9f60647c9c40557d8127317e089761bb87d88a6ff99e541a3c1f5" :answer/id #uuid "5a1b6853-f671-56b2-942a-ed305c9709fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85280/selection/94ee027a688ca91424939a0f105bbb941a9fc5dced1e94113427147a36e2ec8f" :answer/id #uuid "2686c2af-97ef-53be-9b89-32d096597c41" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85280/selection/e7cdcda4251a97af511bdcf58d44eab4f30fd0b2b0441da7c884c63b9240aa9b" :answer/id #uuid "dd538d59-aee9-5725-9d19-809e0e6efe7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{71}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85280/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "39d66f4d-9368-5ee8-b3ff-937e9e295561" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-85280/selection/e7cdcda4251a97af511bdcf58d44eab4f30fd0b2b0441da7c884c63b9240aa9b" :answer-field/choices ["q-85280/selection/6cf1ba8fcecf7ff74fcb48e7f39a2cabed6551908e76b1f229ada74cff9b7d1c" "q-85280/selection/4a34f9e3c0a8895beb5f43363c365da07fa4da2c2c1f10b145640ba26f13b959" "q-85280/selection/d21cf04195b9f60647c9c40557d8127317e089761bb87d88a6ff99e541a3c1f5" "q-85280/selection/94ee027a688ca91424939a0f105bbb941a9fc5dced1e94113427147a36e2ec8f" "q-85280/selection/e7cdcda4251a97af511bdcf58d44eab4f30fd0b2b0441da7c884c63b9240aa9b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-85280" :question/id #uuid "e9a1e571-b70e-5954-9090-bf26c2e1d803" :question/math-academy-id "q-85280" :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}8 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 6\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}22 \\\\ 44\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}10 \\\\ 20 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 17\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 \\\\ -11 \\\\ -9 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{(-9)^{2}+(-11)^{2}+(-9)^{2}+{1}^{2}}=2\\sqrt{71}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-85280/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5924037a-ddc3-5f50-b4b0-1bd813cef255"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-85280"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-41783"] :question/problem "Consider the system $Ax=b$, where the matrix $A$ and the vector $b$ are given below. Given that the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, find the minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖.$\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we write the given system in the form $Ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{A}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\overset{x}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]}} & =\\overset{b}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]}} \\\\ Ax & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can find the least-squares solution $\\overset{^}{x}$ of the equation above by solving the corresponding normal equation, which we obtain by left-multiplying our original equation by ${A}^{T}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{T}\\,A\\overset{^}{x} & ={A}^{T}b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $A$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${A}^{T}\\,A$ is invertible. Therefore, multiplying by $({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}$ on the left, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{x} & =({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}{A}^{T}b \\\\  & ={([\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -20\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe minimum value of $‖Ax-b‖$ is attained when $x=\\overset{^}{x},$ which is the least-squares solution of the system $Ax=b.$ In this case, $A\\overset{^}{x}$ represents the orthogonal projection of $b$ onto the column space of $A,$ and $‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖$ is the distance between the vector $b$ and the column space. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\overset{^}{x}-b & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n‖A\\overset{^}{x}-b‖=\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(-3)^{2}+(-4)^{2}+(-1)^{2}}=\\sqrt{30}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-85278/selection/d18a8dda3b8d65edd15eaec58978ca4092dbb278a54eb8bcb12a2dd65a77d732" :answer/id #uuid "f041f736-504c-56b6-b081-af4086f28b9e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{14}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85278/selection/8ecf42d61d85deb9b7e1281c4e31fb1576dec9e17aa5c6cf3d61e8c06707283b" :answer/id #uuid "949d9cad-94d2-57b5-8cb1-9144e23bc938" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\sqrt{111}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-85278/selection/33a3f214